; Two clipping ScrollContainers whose clip rects land on quarter pixels, one at
; a whole-pixel origin and one at a fractional one.
;
; The one variable: whether a canvas clip is cut at a WHOLE pixel derived from
; the clip rect's position and size rounded SEPARATELY. Godot's
; `RendererCanvasCull::_cull_canvas_item` rounds `final_clip_rect.position` and
; `final_clip_rect.size` independently before either reaches a scissor, so the
; kept columns are `[round(pos), round(pos) + round(size))` — which is NOT
; `round(pos + size)` once `pos` carries a fraction.
;
; A Control's own draw transform is snapped to whole pixels
; (`Control::_update_canvas_item_transform`), so an ordinary Control's clip
; origin is already integral and the two roundings collapse into one. Reaching
; the fraction at all therefore needs a scaled ancestor: `Scaled` is an
; unrotated free Control at the whole (600, 340) with `scale = 1.25`, and 1.25
; times a whole number is exact in binary, so every world coordinate below is a
; quarter pixel with no float slack.
;
; `Whole` is the control: origin (20, 20) — integral — with size
; (500.25, 280.75), so its right edge is 520.25 and its bottom 300.75. Rounding
; position and size separately is indistinguishable from rounding the edge here.
;
; `Fractional` is 322 x 195 at (79, 17) inside `Scaled`, i.e. world origin
; (698.75, 361.25) and world size (402.5, 243.75). Its right edge is x = 1101.25
; with a .75 origin fraction and a .5 size fraction — the pair where
; `round(698.75) + round(402.5) = 699 + 403 = 1102` overshoots
; `round(1101.25) = 1101` by a whole column. Its bottom edge is y = 605.0 with a
; .25 origin fraction and a .75 size fraction, the pair where the two roundings
; still agree, so the same node brackets the divergence instead of only
; demonstrating it.
;
; Both scroll modes are SHOW_NEVER (3) on both containers: the fill overflows
; and is clipped, but no scrollbar chrome is drawn, so every boundary pixel here
; is the clip and nothing else — no stylebox feather, no bar origin snap. Both
; are scrolled 40 px on both axes as well — 50 world px inside `Scaled` — which
; is what lifts the fill's OWN top and left edges out of the clip; unscrolled
; they would sit exactly on the clip's top and left, and a fill edge on a
; fraction is a rasterisation question, not a clipping one. The fills are bright
; against a near-black backdrop so a single kept or dropped column is the
; largest step the scene can make.
;
; Measured against Godot 4.6.3 at `--mode 2d`, backdrop rgb(20, 23, 31).
; `Whole`, fill rgb(242, 230, 64): at y = 150, x = 518/519 are fill and
; x = 520/521 backdrop; at x = 260, y = 299/300 are fill and y = 301/302
; backdrop. `Fractional`, fill rgb(102, 217, 242): at y = 480, x = 1100/1101 are
; fill and x = 1102/1103 backdrop; at x = 900, y = 603/604 are fill and
; y = 605/606 backdrop. No partial column or row at any of the four boundaries.
;
; A Control's own draw transform is pixel-snapped, so a clipper without a scaled
; ancestor cannot reach a fractional clip origin at all — and at a whole origin
; separate rounding and edge rounding cannot be told apart. A clip that rounds
; the edge instead of the position and the size is therefore invisible in every
; scene that does not do what this one does.

[gd_scene format=3]

